# AI team operating principles

The parent session coordinates `.cursor/agents/`. Specialists return work to the parent. They do not spawn other agents. Follow `docs/ai-team/WORKFLOW.md`.

## Repository

This checkout is the AI team. Each application lives in its own `PROJECT_ROOT`, outside this repository, with its own Git history. Paths are in `docs/ai-team/PROJECT_LAYOUT.md`. Ordinary application work must not change agents, skills, rules, templates, or these team docs. Ask the user before changing them.

## General

- Read the existing code and docs before changing them.
- Keep the repository's technology choices. Leave working code in place when it already meets the task.
- Apply SOLID and small modules where they fit the current design.
- Add a dependency only when the approved design requires it.
- Do not invent requirements. Record assumptions in the deliverable.
- Prefer the smallest maintainable change.
- Update docs that the change makes stale.

## Collaboration

- Give every task one owner, one status, and explicit inputs and outputs.
- Use the handoff in `docs/ai-team/AGENT_HANDOFF_PROTOCOL.md`.
- Run tasks in parallel only when their files and dependencies are independent.
- Local subagents share this checkout. Run overlapping or dependent edits one at a time. Ask the user before using a cloud subagent or any other isolated worktree.
- The parent reviews deliverables and updates `PROJECT_ROOT/docs/development/TASK_BOARD.md`. A specialist reply is not completion.

## Done

Mark a task DONE only when `docs/ai-team/DEFINITION_OF_DONE.md` is satisfied: the deliverable exists, required checks were run, acceptance criteria hold, required docs are updated, and limitations are recorded.

## Safety

- Never write production secret values. Name the environment variable. A local demo account may be documented only when its password is development-only.
- Never delete production data. Wait for the user to approve the exact destructive command.
- Never present the application for review unless it has started and the local URL was checked.
- Never treat passing tests as approval of the UI.
- Never treat silence as approval.
- Never deploy or run a production command until the user has approved the running local application and then explicitly approved that production action.
- Read an existing file before writing it. Preserve content the task does not assign you to change.
- Never claim tests passed without a command and its result.
- Never initialize an application Git repository inside the AI team repository.
- Never push, publish, or create a GitHub repository until the user explicitly approves that action.

## Gates

- Gate 1: the user approves requirements before research is used for design.
- Show the consolidated research to the user before architecture starts.
- Gate 2: the user approves the architecture before implementation tasks start.
- Gate 3: run the application locally after QA and stop. The user must explicitly approve that running application.
- Gate 4: production preparation starts only after Gate 3 is open. Deploy only after the user explicitly approves the production action.

Details: `docs/ai-team/APPROVAL_POLICY.md`.
